course: dictionary
lesson: 13340
title: Habitually: doing it again and again
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Habitually comes from the word habit, which means something you do regularly. If you habitually do something, you do it again and again, as part of how you normally behave. «Mia habitually forgets her lunch box on Mondays.» This means Mia often forgets it, every week, not just once.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Words like always, usually, every day and again and again tell you the action happens often. «Sam is habitually late for school, and he always runs up the hill.» Late is the problem, and always tells you it happens again and again.
explain: Use the swap check. If you can put usually or always into the sentence and it still makes sense, habitually probably means the same. «She habitually tidies her room.» becomes «She usually tidies her room.» That makes sense, so habitually means regularly, as a habit.
fact: Habitually means doing something regularly, as a habit, again and again.
fact: Habitually means usually or always, because that is how someone normally behaves.
fact: Habitually is an adverb: it tells you how often or how someone does something.
fact: «Habitually late» describes someone who is late again and again, not just once.
fact: Habit is the noun, and habitually is the adverb made from it.
exercise: «Ben habitually brushes his teeth before bed.» What does habitually mean here? | Never | Quickly and loudly | Once, by accident | *Regularly, as a habit
exercise: «The bus habitually arrives late on rainy days.» What does habitually suggest? | It never happens | It happens only in summer | *It happens often, as a habit | It happened only once
exercise: Which sentence shows a habit? | Nia read one book last summer | *Nia habitually reads a book before bed | Nia finished her book in one day | Nia lost her book at school
test: «Grandma habitually puts the kettle on at four o'clock.» What does this tell you? | Grandma did it once, by mistake | Grandma is too busy to make tea | *Grandma does it every day, as a routine | Grandma does not like tea
test: Which word could replace habitually without changing the meaning? | suddenly | rarely | *usually | never
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to habitually? | *usually | suddenly | quietly | angrily
